{
  "abstract": "We study nine-colorings of ordinary finite covers of the ten-chromatic source $G_{25}$, allowing arbitrary internal host, arm and center matchings. We select every vertex in one large-arm fiber and absorb two small arms around certain rigid selected-head triangles. All selected large rows retain five available colors; deleting them leaves a cubic graph. Each residual $K_4$ needs one matching-agreement row, whereas every other residual component extends from three-lists with no agreement assumption. We give five compatible boundary repairs coordinated by one auxiliary graph. An induced-density inequality forces its three-core to be cubic, and a source preference excludes $K_4$, so Brooks' theorem supplies simultaneous boundary colors. A further operation handles coherent small complementary hosts whenever their four-host graph has a missing edge. Promoting one complementary host and identifying it with a nonadjacent selected head yields a greedy five-list extension that preserves every exterior head color. A second source preference coordinates arbitrarily many such operations. Together with an explicitly imported 17-vertex nonbiplanarity certificate, this gives a sufficient theorem for biplanar covers satisfying stated coherence, alignment and residual-clique conditions. A connected 575-vertex control exercises all operations, including a residual component with twelve actual three-lists and no agreement rows. Independent checks verify all 3,082 edges and exact list and incidence invariants; the imported compact certificate also passes fresh semantic, exposure, RUP and LRAT checks. The infinite claims follow from hand proofs. These results restrict the ordinary-cover route but do not resolve unrestricted covers or Earth–Moon.",
